import RequestProject.Wikidata.Lens
import RequestProject.Epistemic.Quotient

/-!
# One-way determination is not equivalence

`RequestProject.Wikidata.Lens` already shows that the reified RDF layer
determines the direct layer while the direct layer does not determine the
reified one, and that there is no lens transition in either direction.  This
module isolates the mathematical reason, in a form that is not about RDF at all
and can be reused for any projection.

A **reconstruction** of a projection `proj : Fine → Coarse` is a map back that
recovers every fine state on the nose.  `no_reconstruction_of_identified` says
that a projection identifying two distinct states admits no reconstruction —
whatever the reconstruction is allowed to look at, and however cleverly it is
chosen.  Contrapositively, `injective_of_reconstruction`: reconstructible
projections are exactly the injective ones.

This is strictly stronger than saying that one particular candidate inverse
fails, and it is what separates *determination* from *equivalence*: the fine
state always determines its projection, so determination in one direction is
free; a section of the projection (`Epistemic.SectionedProjection`) is also free
and still does not reconstruct fine states
(`Epistemic.exists_section_not_injective`).  What fails is reconstruction.

Two instantiations follow.  `no_reconstruction_of_forgetRank`: the map that
forgets a statement's rank is not reconstructible, because a normal-rank and a
preferred-rank statement have the same subject, property and value.
`no_reconstruction_of_directTriples`: the direct-RDF projection of a whole
knowledge base is not reconstructible, using the rank witness of
`Wikidata.direct_layer_forgets_rank`.
-/

